course: dictionary
lesson: 968
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning the writer means. Bluff is a good example. In «It was just a bluff», it is a trick. In «a bluff old farmer», it describes a friendly, direct person.
explain: Look at the context. In «She tried to bluff her way through the test», the word bluff goes with trying to fool someone, so it means a trick. In «The house stood on a bluff above the river», bluff means a steep cliff or high bank.
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence. Swap in your meaning and check that it still makes sense. If the words are about cards or games, bluff is a trick. If they are about a steep slope, bluff is a cliff.
fact: Bluff can be a verb, a noun or an adjective: you bluff, it is a bluff, or he is a bluff old farmer.
fact: «Call someone's bluff» means to make them prove what they claim.
fact: As a verb, «bluff» means to pretend you are stronger, braver or cleverer than you really are, in order to trick someone.
fact: As a noun, «a bluff» can also be a steep cliff or high bank, especially by a river or the sea.
fact: «We had not considered that» is more honest than bluffing, as the judges' example shows.
exercise: «Sam had no good cards, but he kept betting as if he did. It was just a bluff.» What does bluff mean? | *A trick of pretending to be stronger than you are | A friendly, loud way of talking | A high cliff by the river | A kind of dice game
exercise: «The walkers stood on the bluff and looked down at the river.» What is a bluff here? | A small boat | A trick | *A steep cliff or high bank | Loud and friendly
exercise: In «a bluff old farmer», what does bluff mean? | To trick someone with a pretend card | A steep river bank | *Friendly and honest, but rather direct and loud | Clever and quiet
test: «Call someone's bluff» means: | To laugh at their jokes | *To make them prove what they claim | To run away from them | To pay them money
test: Which sentence uses bluff as a noun meaning a trick? | The bluff stood over the sea | She tried to bluff the teacher | *«It was just a bluff» | He spoke in a bluff, cheerful way
exam: Which phrase means to pretend you know more than you really do? | A steep bluff by the river | A bluff old farmer | Call someone's bluff | *Bluff your way through
